# TypeScript/JavaScript (Node.js) patterns

Design patterns and anti-patterns for Node.js services, libraries and CLIs in
TypeScript or JavaScript; component composition belongs to the `react` pack.

## Idiomatic patterns

- Validate external input (HTTP body, CLI args, env vars, file content) at the boundary where it enters the process, with the project's schema library (`zod`, `valibot`, `ajv`) or explicit checks if it has none.
- Inject (constructor/factory parameters) anything that talks to the outside world (DB client, HTTP client, clock, RNG); no global singleton in code that must be testable.
- Prefer small composable functions over a class hierarchy when there is no shared mutable state; use a class when holding state across calls (connection pool, cache).
- Use `map`/`filter`/`reduce`/`flatMap` for data transforms instead of index loops with push, unless profiling shows a hot loop's allocation cost matters.
- For an operation with an expected failure mode, return a discriminated union (`{ ok: true, value } | { ok: false, error }`) or throw a typed error class; do not use `null`/`undefined`/`-1` for both "not found" and "failed".
- Use `EventEmitter`/`AsyncIterator` for a stream of events over time, not a single async result.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- God module / barrel re-export cycle: an `index.ts` re-exporting a package that is imported back into one of its modules.
- An `async function` whose body is entirely synchronous CPU work with no `await`.
- `new Promise(...)` around a callback API that has `node:util` `promisify` or a `.promises`/`fs/promises` variant.
- Mutating a shared config/options object in place instead of returning a new one.
- Deep relative import chains (`../../../../lib/x`) across module boundaries instead of a package-level export.
- A try/catch that only rethrows the same error unchanged; add context (`cause`, a wrapped error type) or remove it.
